  "narrative": "The newly delivered independent validations materially strengthen the central dataset-level associations. For KEAP1 loss, the original discovery association (signed estimate +0.832, 95% interval +0.766 to +0.897) is reproduced in the independent validation split as +0.826, with a conservative independent interval +0.735 to +0.917. Because the claim was positive-direction, this means KEAP1-loss cells have higher dependency_composite values, i.e. weaker composite dependency, by a very large margin above the 0.15-unit threshold. The response deadline is therefore resolved with strong independent support. For SMARCA4 loss, the original discovery claim for a negative raw association was signed-positive +0.832, corresponding to a raw decrease in dependency_composite. The independent validation gives signed +0.845, conservative interval +0.752 to +0.939, meaning SMARCA4-loss cells have substantially lower, more negative dependency scores, i.e. stronger composite dependency. This validation confirms that the SMARCA4-loss association is not a discovery-split artifact. The high-screen-quality subgroup H32 is also independently validated: signed +0.809, conservative interval +0.691 to +0.927. Thus SMARCA4-loss-associated stronger dependency remains robust among higher-quality screens. I accept and close the main-effect investigations for H7, H8, and H32, while leaving separate context/interaction claims available for future work if they remain informative. Combined with prior evidence, PTEN loss remains accepted as a negative raw association, i.e. PTEN-loss cells have more negative dependency scores, although some context-specific independence analyses have lower bounds slightly below the 0.15 threshold. TP53 loss remains accepted as a positive raw association, i.e. weaker composite dependency, but it has not yet been independently validated. The current supported landscape is therefore dominated by four large or moderately large within-dataset associations: KEAP1 loss positive/positive composite dependency, SMARCA4 loss negative/stronger dependency, PTEN loss negative/stronger dependency, and TP53 loss positive/weaker dependency. These are associations in the supplied dataset, not causal effects and not claims of external generalizability. Negative findings include KRAS mutation, MYC amplification, EGFR amplification, PIK3CA mutation, ARID1A loss, STK11 loss in the registered negative direction, RB1 loss, SMAD4 loss, MTAP loss when excluding TP53 loss, and the three constructed research markers, which were null or below the meaningful threshold. Unresolved issues remain for TP53-loss context and independent validation, PTEN-loss independence from KEAP1 and SMARCA4, CDKN2A loss near the threshold, and several interactions and untested broad predictors. The most informative next analyses are the registered broad predictors and the new focused TP53 and PTEN context contrasts: TP53 loss in high-quality and adenocarcinoma contexts after removing KEAP1 and SMARCA4, and PTEN loss in high-quality screens after removing KEAP1 and SMARCA4. In the next appraise, if validation budget is used, independent validation of TP53 loss or CDKN2A loss would be the highest-value candidates, depending on whether the priority is strengthening an already accepted moderate effect or resolving a near-threshold unresolved effect.",
  "research_notes": "Iteration 3 synthesis update: Independent validation has now settled the main-effect status of KEAP1 loss (H7), SMARCA4 loss (H8), and the SMARCA4 high-screen-quality subgroup (H32). KEAP1 loss is accepted as a large positive association with composite dependency score (weaker dependency), robust and independently validated. SMARCA4 loss is accepted as a large negative raw association with composite dependency score (stronger dependency), robust across discovery exclusions, subtype, screen quality, and independent validation. PTEN loss remains accepted overall as a negative raw association, with independent validation supporting the point estimate, although the validation lower bound is below the 0.15 threshold and independence from KEAP1/SMARCA4 in individual strata remains somewhat unresolved. TP53 loss is accepted as a positive/weaker-dependency association and is robust to exclusion of KEAP1 and SMARCA4 individually and jointly, but it is not independently validated. CDKN2A loss remains unresolved near the 0.15 threshold. Next priority is to analyze untested broad predictors (ERBB2 amplification, BRCA2 loss, NF1 loss, MSI-high) and the new TP53/PTEN context refinements while keeping interaction and quality-control claims secondary. If requesting a validation next, choose either TP53 main effect to strengthen an accepted large association or CDKN2A main effect to resolve a near-threshold claim. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied dataset, not causal or externally generalizable effects."
